---
description: SelectStatement uses entity type names; mappings produce datasource names
alwaysApply: true
---

# Select names match the entity type

A `SelectStatement` names PascalCase entity types and properties: `Contact`, `ContactType`, `Contact.FirstName`, `ContactType.Name`. Do not put datasource table or column names in the statement. Do not alias a selected column to snake_case.

`EntityMapper` maps the entity type to the table (`Contact` → `contacts_base`; other types through the repository’s snake table mapper, e.g. `ContactType` → `contact_type`). `PropertyMappings` map properties to columns (`FirstName` → `first_name`, `ContactTypeId` → `contact_type_id`).

Use Pascal `AS` aliases only when the same property name would collide across a join (for example `Contact.Id AS Id` and `Address.Id AS AddressId`).

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — datasource names and snake aliases in the statement
new SelectStatement(new()
{
    Table = "contacts_base",
    Fields = ["contacts_base.first_name AS first_name", "contact_type.name AS contact_type_name"],
    Joins = [Join.Inner("contact_type", "contacts_base.contact_type_id", "contact_type.id")],
});

// ✅ GOOD — entity names; repository mappings compile to the datasource
new SelectStatement(new()
{
    Table = "Contact",
    Fields = ["Contact.FirstName AS FirstName", "ContactType.Name AS ContactTypeName"],
    Joins = [Join.Inner("ContactType", "Contact.ContactTypeId", "ContactType.Id")],
});
```
